Jahshaka patch 0070 — the VCT cone's start bias is ONE CELL, whichever way the
surface faces (MEDIA ONLY)

WHAT IS WRONG UPSTREAM
----------------------
Every voxel-cone-tracing cone — the six diffuse ones and the specular one, in the
pixel piece, and the diffuse set again in the bounce-injection compute twin —
starts a short distance off the surface so that it does not immediately sample
the voxel the surface itself occupies. Upstream expresses that distance as

    posLS += (dirLS / maxVal) * vctInvResolution * lerp( 15.0f, 1.0f, maxVal )

where `dirLS` is the geometric normal in probe space and `maxVal` is its largest
component. For a normal pointing down an axis maxVal = 1 and the step is ONE
voxel. For a body-diagonal normal maxVal = 1/sqrt(3) = 0.577, so lerp(15,1,0.577)
= 6.92 and the division by maxVal makes it TWELVE voxels — 8 cm against 94 cm on
a 7.8 cm cascade. The compute twin has the same shape without the lerp (it
divides the normal by its largest component before stepping one inverse
resolution): one voxel to 1.73.

The stated reason is staircase aliasing on axis-aligned steps, and on a BOX it is
invisible: every face has maxVal = 1 and every cone starts one voxel out.

On a CURVED surface it is a seam by construction. A sphere's normal sweeps every
direction, so the start point travels from one voxel to twelve and back across
the six arcs where two components of the normal are equal in magnitude. A
downward-diagonal normal on the lower half of a sphere starts its specular cone
most of a metre INSIDE the floor and reads the floor's contact shadow; the
upward-diagonal normal beside it starts above the sphere's own lit voxels and
reads those. The result is a bright warped streak beside a dark smear, curved
along the arc.

That is the artefact the owner's smoke of push #41 reported on a plain sphere in
an empty scene (spikes/smoke-41/screenshot7.png) — at the document's default
roughness both the screen-space and the ray-traced reflection arms are gated off,
so this cone IS the whole reflection of that surface.

THE FIX
-------
A start bias is a geometric statement — "leave the surface's own voxel before
sampling" — and a geometric statement cannot depend on how the surface happens to
sit against the lattice. So the step becomes ONE CELL along the normal, measured
along the normal, everywhere:

    biasDirLS = normalise( dirLS )
    posLS    += biasDirLS * vctInvResolution.xyz        // COMPONENTWISE

THE STEP IS COMPONENTWISE AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF "ONE CELL".
`vctInvResolution_cascadeMaxLod[i].xyz` is the inverse RESOLUTION per axis, so
multiplying a UNIT direction by it componentwise gives a displacement whose
length is exactly one cell in every direction on an isotropic volume, and the
natural per-axis cell on an anisotropic one. The obvious-looking scalar
alternative — `dot( abs( d ), invRes )`, "the cell size along this direction" —
is the L1 norm: one cell down an axis and sqrt(3) = 1.73 cells along a body
diagonal. That is a smaller sweep than upstream's twelve but the SAME defect, and
a sphere still bands along the same six arcs. It is also exactly the form the
bounce-injection compute twin already uses (`geomNormal * p_invVoxelResolution`),
so after this the two marches agree.

The same expression replaces the same term at the two cascade HOPS in the pixel
piece (the diffuse walk and the specular walk each re-enter the next cascade one
bias along the normal, in that cascade's units), and in the compute shader.

WHAT IT MEASURES (all on this box, RTX 4080 SUPER / 595.84; the A/B swaps ONLY
this media file on one binary, each arm with a cold shader cache)
---------------------------------------------------------------
  * Photon OFF: ZERO pixels change. The whole effect is inside the cone.
  * The owner's own pose, gradient statistics over a fixed disc inside the
    sphere (n = 70,679 px): max luminance step 32 -> 21, p99.9 27 -> 18,
    p99 18 -> 11, p95 9 -> 4, MEAN 2.592 -> 1.876 output codes. The chevron
    crease and its dark notch are gone; what is left at the sphere's lower rim is
    the contact shadow against the floor, which a one-cell bias is SUPPOSED to
    see (the old bias floated the cone over it).
  * The SMOKE-41 S4 recipe with the irradiance field OFF, where these cones are
    the whole diffuse answer: max band step 25 -> 10, p99.9 15 -> 8, p99 9 -> 4,
    mean 1.522 -> 1.069.
  * With the field ON at Epic the cage hides most of the banding either way:
    8.6 % of pixels move, by at most 14/255.
  * The engine selftest is BYTE-IDENTICAL (1fd91da9...): the default scene's only
    lit surface is a horizontal ground, whose normal is axis-aligned and which
    therefore always got exactly one cell.

